			"Message": "docs(Echo): Add work-stealing architecture diagram\n\nIntroduce a Mermaid graph visualization in Documentation/GitHub/Architecture.md to illustrate the internal data flow of the Echo task scheduler. The diagram maps the relationship between the Task Layer, Queue Layer (crossbeam-deque), and the Scheduler Layer, depicting the multi-worker topology and the priority-based (High, Normal, Low) work-stealing mechanics.\n\nThis visual aid complements the existing textual documentation, providing contributors with an immediate overview of how tasks flow from ApplicationRunTime (Mountain) into the worker pools and how inter-worker stealing occurs for load balancing.",
			"SHA": "6f6e8f9946fa9bd0120be02b046eca88cbcfed92"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-15T22:23:48Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				57,
				1
			],
			"Message": "docs(Echo): Add comprehensive architectural overview for work-stealing scheduler\n\nCreate a new `Documentation/GitHub/Architecture.md` file to serve as the central technical reference for the Echo crate. This document details the bounded work-stealing design, priority-based scheduling (High, Normal, Low), and the lock-free queue implementation using `crossbeam-deque`.\n\nThe guide covers the three core subsystems: Task Layer, Scheduler Layer, and Queue Layer, including module structure mapping and the `SchedulerBuilder` configuration API. It also explains the integration with Mountain's `ApplicationRunTime` and Common's `ActionEffect` system, alongside performance benchmarks and the work-stealing mechanics for multi-threaded efficiency.\n\nThis addition provides contributors and maintainers with a definitive map of the high-performance execution engine.",
			"SHA": "1c47d91d2356f7b7b7107d85e6e2739cf48a15ab"
		},
